sentences.t:
ὦ Κίρκη, τίς γάρ κεν ἀνήρ, ὃς ἐναίσιμος εἴη,
πρὶν τλαίη πάσσασθαι ἐδητύος ἠδὲ ποτῆτος,
πρὶν λύσασθ’ ἑτάρους καὶ ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖσιν ἰδέσθαι;

sentences.tr:
ō Kirkē, tis gar ken anēr, hos enaisimos eiē,
prin tlaiē passasthai edētyos ēde potētos,
prin lysasth’ hetarous kai en ophthalmoisin idesthai;

sentences.l:
As when field-dwelling calves, around the cows of the herd
coming to the yard, when they have had their fill of grazing,
all together skip, facing them;

and no longer the pens
hold them, but heavily lowing they run round
their mothers;

and you yourselves, rouse yourselves to follow along with me, all,
so that you may see your companions in the sacred halls of Circe
drinking and eating;

who, for her, the great house would guard, even by necessity,
as the Cyclops did, when into his mid-yard came
our companions, and with them the bold Odysseus followed;

Meanwhile the other companions in the halls Circe
kindly bathed, and anointed richly with oil,
and around them then thick cloaks threw, and tunics;
